  "output": "## Reality check first\n\nBefore the category-by-category breakdown: this repo is much earlier-stage than AGENTS.md's target architecture. I read the actual files rather than assuming the spec is built:\n\n- **`engine/`** — every pipeline function is a stub. `pipeline/blocking.py`, `pipeline/detect.py`, `pipeline/normalize.py`, `db.py`, `agent/graph.py`, `agent/tools.py`, `agent/prompts.py`, all three connectors, and both `eval/generate.py` / `eval/harness.py` functions are `raise NotImplementedError(\"Phase N — see AGENTS.md § ...\")`. The only real logic in the whole engine is two pure one-liners (`should_auto_apply`, `severity_for_orphan`) — nothing to optimize there.\n- **`web/`** — only the landing page, `/get-started`, the console shell, and `/sources` (list view) exist. There is no conflict queue, no runs list, no polling, and grepping the whole `web/` tree turns up **zero** `useEffect`, `useState`, `setInterval`, `setTimeout`, `addEventListener`, or `subscribe` calls. TanStack Query is installed but not wired to anything yet.\n\nSo there are no real N+1 query loops, render-thrash, subscriptions, or leaks to find — that code doesn't exist yet. Inventing findings against stub functions would be noise. Below is what's actually there, plus the handful of things worth locking in now, before the stubbed logic gets filled in.\n\n## 1. N+1 query / redundant computation — real, today\n\n**`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23`** — `countSourcesBySide()` re-fetches and re-parses the entire `sources` table just to produce three counts:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();     // SELECT * + Zod-parse every row\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nPush the grouping into Postgres:\n\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const [row] = await getDb()\n    .select({\n      crm: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where kind in ('salesforce','hubspot'))`,\n      warehouse: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where kind in ('databricks','snowflake'))`,\n      synthetic: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where kind = 'synthetic')`,\n    })\n    .from(sources);\n  return row;\n}\n```\n\nLow urgency at single-tenant scale with a handful of sources, but it's the one place a \"load everything, filter in JS\" pattern is already baked in rather than something that grew organically — cheap to fix now while it's one function.\n\n*(Note: `web/.claude-flow/metrics/performance.json` in this repo already contains this exact finding from a prior automated pass — I verified it independently by reading the source, not by trusting that file.)*\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders — none found\n\nEvery \"use client\" component (`NavRail`, `LandingHero`, `PipelineStrip`, `StepList`, `GetStartedHero`, `SourcesEmptyState`, `SignOutControl`) is a one-shot entrance animation (`opacity`/`y`, 120ms) with no local state, no effects, and no derived values expensive enough to warrant `useMemo`/`useCallback`. `NavRail` recomputes `active` per nav item on pathname change, but that's a 5-item array — memoizing it would be the premature-abstraction AGENTS.md tells you not to do. Nothing to fix here yet; revisit once the conflict queue (`j`/`k` keyboard nav over ~400 rows, per the design system spec) is built, since that's where a real re-render cost will first show up.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities — architecture is already right, just unimplemented\n\nThe one caching decision that matters most for cost (`AGENTS.md` § Model Selection) is already correctly *designed* in `engine/agent/prompts.py`:\n\n```python\ndef build_ruleset_block(rules: list[SurvivorshipRule]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The cached prefix. Must be byte-identical across a run.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError(...)\n\ndef build_conflict_block(conflict: Conflict) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The per-call suffix. Everything that varies lives here.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError(...)\n```\n\nWhen you implement this: `build_ruleset_block` must be **deterministic** (stable rule ordering, canonical JSON — not dict-iteration order) or the prompt-cache prefix silently stops matching between calls and you pay full price every time without any error telling you. Worth a unit test that calls it twice with the same rules and asserts byte-equality.\n\nNo other caching gaps exist yet — there's no repeated computation to cache because there's no computation.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nNo intervals, no manual subscriptions, no event listeners anywhere in `web/`. `web/lib/db/index.ts` correctly caches the Postgres connection on `globalThis` to survive Next.js HMR reloads and uses `prepare: false` for Supabase's transaction pooler — that's the standard fix for the leak this exact setup usually causes, already applied. Nothing to flag.\n\n## 5. Forward-looking, grounded in the actual stubs\n\nThese aren't bugs — they're the places the current function signatures make it easy to do the *wrong* thing later, worth keeping in mind when Phase 2–4 get implemented:\n\n- **`engine/pipeline/blocking.py`** already frames the O(n·k)-not-O(n²) constraint correctly (\"Blocking exists so the pipeline is O(n·k) not O(n²). NEVER compare all pairs\"). The risk is a naive first-pass implementation of `match()` that does exact-key matching correctly but falls back to a nested `for a in side_a: for b in side_b` loop for the leftover unmatched records instead of routing them through the FAISS `top_k` index. Worth a regression test with a few thousand synthetic entities that fails if match time is superlinear.\n- **`engine/db.py`** — `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` already take `list[...]`, i.e., batch-shaped signatures, which heads off the classic per-row-INSERT N+1 write. When implemented, use `psycopg`'s `executemany`/`COPY`, not a Python loop issuing one INSERT per row.\n- **`web/lib/db/schema.ts`** — only `entities` has an index (`runId, sourceId, externalId`). `matches`, `conflicts`, `resolutions`, and `audit_log` have none beyond their primary keys. Once the conflict queue (Phase 4, item 13: \"filterable by severity and status\") exists, every query will filter `conflicts` by `runId` and join to `matches`/`resolutions`. Add these now, before real data accumulates and a migration means a table rewrite:\n\n```ts\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { ... },\n  (table) => [index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId)]);\n\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { ... },\n  (table) => [\n    index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n    index(\"conflicts_match_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n  ]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { ... },\n  (table) => [index(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId)]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { ... },\n  (table) => [index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId)]);\n```\n\n**Bottom line:** nothing urgent. The one real fix today is `countSourcesBySide` (small, optional). The rest is guidance to bake in as you implement the Phase 2–4 stubs, so the N+1/caching/index problems this kind of audit usually finds never get written in the first place.\n",
